This is the shared **work directory** for the project. The bare git repository
lives at `{{ _wt_repo }}`; this directory's `.git` file points at it, so you can
run git here to **look things up** — but you cannot edit anything from here (the
pad reports as a bare repo, so there is no working tree).

## Looking things up

Run git directly in this directory against the live repo:

```bash
git log --oneline -20 main              # recent history
git branch                              # what work is in flight
git show main:path/to/file              # read a file at a ref
git log --oneline <category>/<branch>   # someone's in-progress branch
```

## Doing work → use a worktree

Don't edit files here. Each piece of work gets its own worktree at
`{{ _wt_workdir }}/<category>/<branch>` on a branch of the same name,
`<category>/<branch>` — exactly what a plain `git worktree add` there would use.

Claude users: ask Claude to call the **`EnterWorktree`** tool (or start with
`claude --worktree <category>/<branch>` from the CLI). Both routes go through
the machine-wide `WorktreeCreate` hook (`/etc/claude/hooks/worktree-create`,
deployed by the `claude_code` role), which creates the worktree and its branch.

The name is **`<category>/<branch>`**:

- `<category>` is just a folder under this work directory grouping related work.
  There's no allowlist — a new category folder is created on demand. This
  project starts with: {% for c in _wt_categories %}`{{ c }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last else "" }}{% endfor %}.
- `<branch>` is a URL slug: lowercase `a-z`/`0-9`, start with a letter, contain
  at least one `-`, at most 40 characters — e.g. `add-dns-role`, `fix-login`.

So: `feature/add-dns-role`, `fix/login-redirect`, `experiment/new-router`.

### Choosing a base

A new worktree's branch is always cut from **`main`**, wherever you create it
from. That keeps unrelated work out of your branch and your eventual MR
reviewable.

Before starting, decide whether `main` is the right base for *this* work:

- **Does other, unmerged work belong in it?** If your change builds on an open
  MR or another feature branch, you want that work present. Either base the new
  branch on it directly (pass that branch instead of `main` to
  `git worktree add … -b …`), or start from `main` and **merge the other branch
  in** once you're in the worktree (`git merge <branch>`).
- **Is it independent?** Then plain `main` is right — leave it.

`EnterWorktree` always gives you a `main`-based worktree, so to build on another
branch pull it in once you're inside:

```bash
git merge <category>/<other-branch>
```

Prefer the smallest base that gets the job done: merging a branch in is explicit
and visible in the log, whereas basing on it silently pulls its (possibly
in-review) history into yours.

## Patterns we work by

This project has a written pattern library, and a copy of it is installed on
this machine at **`{{ worktrees_patterns_dir }}`** — read it there, no network
needed:

- **`{{ worktrees_patterns_dir }}/patterns.md`** and `patterns/` — how we prefer
  to do things and why, grouped into `workflows/`, `development/`,
  `operation/`, `about/`, `meta/`.
- **`{{ worktrees_patterns_dir }}/anti-patterns.md`** and `anti-patterns/` —
  practices that look reasonable and aren't, each pointing at what to do
  instead.

Before designing something non-trivial, check whether a pattern already covers
it — branching and merge flow, deployment shape, issue tracking, and the
worktree layout you're standing in are all written down there. Prefer the
project's existing pattern over a fresh invention, and say which one you're
following.

```bash
ls {{ worktrees_patterns_dir }}/patterns/
grep -ril <topic> {{ worktrees_patterns_dir }}/patterns/ {{ worktrees_patterns_dir }}/anti-patterns/
```

The copy is read-only and refreshed by Ansible. To change a pattern, edit it in
its repository and open an MR like any other change.

## Who merges what

Open an MR against `main`; the maintainer reviews and merges. Don't push to
`main` from a worktree branch — the bare repo's hooks will reject it.

### Merge requests are empty commits

An MR is an **empty commit on the work branch itself**, subject `MR: <title>`,
body carrying the description. It marks the branch ready for merging —
everything before it is work in flight, the MR commit is the boundary.

The subject may carry a Conventional-Commits scope naming the topic,
`MR(<topic>): <title>`; this worktree's own `<category>/<branch>` name is the
natural topic to use:

```bash
git commit --allow-empty -m "MR(<category>/<branch>): <title>

<description — motivation, summary of changes, anything the maintainer needs.>"
```

List open MRs (branches with an `MR:` commit not yet reachable from `main`):

```bash
git log --branches --not main --grep '^MR[(:]' --oneline
```

**Claude: only create the MR commit once the user says the work is ready for
review.** Committing work to the branch is normal and expected as you go — that
already publishes it. The `MR:` commit is a separate, explicit signal that the
user owns. Don't add it because a task looks finished, tests pass, or you're
wrapping up; wait for the user to say so, or ask.

### You don't need to push

Committing is enough. Worktrees share the bare repo, so a commit updates the
branch there directly — nothing to push for the work to be visible to everyone
else. If the branch is also supposed to reach a remote, the repo's
`reference-transaction` hook pushes it automatically to every remote configured
with `remote.<name>.autopush=true`, in the background, on every ref update.

So: don't run `git push`. If you're wondering whether a mirror push happened,
read the log rather than pushing by hand:

```bash
cat "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/autopush.log"
```

See [MR Commits 💌](https://cute-devops.patterns.how/patterns/workflows/mr-commits)
and [Shared Worktrees 🌳](https://cute-devops.patterns.how/patterns/workflows/shared-worktrees)
for the full patterns.

